Alembic migrations for the Dairy Management System (MySQL 8).

# Revision chain

    0001_baseline        ten core tables (users, buffaloes, customers, ...)
    0002_customer_portal portal accounts, orders, queries, sales.source_order_id
    0003_password_reset  password_reset_tokens
    0004_token_version   users.token_version, customer_portal_accounts.token_version
    0005_security_audit  security_audit_events
    0006_login_lockout   failed-login counters and lock state on both account tables

`0001_baseline` started life as an empty revision that was stamped onto the
legacy Sequelize database. It now carries the real DDL so a fresh volume can be
built from nothing, and every revision is idempotent.

# Why the migrations are idempotent

The same chain has to work against three different databases:

* a brand-new MySQL volume created by `docker compose up`;
* the legacy Sequelize database (tables exist, `alembic_version` may be absent);
* a database left behind by an interrupted `alembic upgrade`.

MySQL has no `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` equivalent for indexes/columns in
Alembic's DDL, so every revision probes the live schema first through
`app/db/migration_helpers.py` (`create_table_if_missing`,
`add_column_if_missing`, `create_index_if_missing`,
`create_foreign_key_if_missing`, and the `drop_*_if_exists` counterparts for
downgrades). Re-running `alembic upgrade head` is therefore a no-op instead of
"table already exists" / "duplicate column name" / "duplicate key name".

Never import another revision file from a migration to "self-heal" the schema
(an earlier version of `0002` re-ran `0001` from disk and duplicated the
baseline DDL). Add a guarded operation instead.

# Commands

Run them from `backend_py/`:

    uv run alembic current           # where the database is
    uv run alembic upgrade head      # apply everything pending
    uv run alembic downgrade -1      # roll back one revision
    uv run alembic history           # the chain

Inside the backend container the virtualenv is already on `PATH`, and the image
does not ship `uv.lock`, so use the plain executable:

    docker compose exec backend alembic current
    docker compose exec backend alembic upgrade head

The container applies migrations by itself on start (`docker-entrypoint.sh`),
so these are only needed for inspection or manual recovery.

# Adding a revision

    uv run alembic revision -m "describe the change"

Write the DDL by hand (do not autogenerate against a live database), and guard
every operation with the helpers above so the revision stays re-runnable.
Never downgrade or reset a production database; take a backup first.
